{
	title: "ASON is the old Hal's ason.ts, verbatim",
	spec: "src/common/ason.ts is a verbatim copy of the old Hal's (tasks/8/ason.ts, identical to ~/.hal/src/utils/ason.ts); nobody edits it, and hal2 adapts to their API instead (the ason object: stringify, parse, parseAll, parseStream, COMMENTS). Comments round-trip: live ASON files that a person edits by hand (config.ason, the credentials file, any liveFile) are parsed with comments kept and written back with them, so a hand-written comment survives every save. Test: a config file with comments on keys and array items keeps them after hal2 changes a value and saves (comment preservation is never dropped).",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"ason.ts is a byte copy of tasks/8 (cmp it). ason.test.ts started as tasks/8/ason.test.ts and is pruned like any test: its tail -f e2e case (and the tail-file.ts helper only it used) is gone, so parseStream is tested with in-memory streams, and it is linted like other files. hal2's old extras live elsewhere: lines.encode (was ason.stringifyLine) and history.load's own line parsing with the torn-tail 'partial' (was parseStream's midRecord/onPartial).",
		'live-file parses with { comments: true } and stringifies smart, so COMMENTS ride on the data objects: Object.assign and spread copy the symbol, a reload deletes it via Reflect.ownKeys. Replacing an array or object from code drops its comments; array comments are by index.'
	],
